Subject: [PATCH] net/tcp: accept cumulative ACK progress after partial replay

The buffered sender rewinds tx_unacked while preparing an RTO replay, but
retains sndseq_max, the highest sequence ever transmitted. With congestion
control, only one MSS may be replayed initially. Inferring the lowest
unacknowledged sequence as sndseq_max - tx_unacked then rejects a valid ACK
for that MSS as old. Recovery retransmits the same bytes until timeout.

Use the latest accepted cumulative ACK, maintained by tcp_snd_wnd_update in
snd_wl2, as the lower bound after connection establishment. Retain the
existing upper bound and handshake accounting. Sequence comparisons remain
wrap-safe; duplicate ACKs remain eligible for loss/window processing.
No buffer, timeout, retry-limit or application workaround is introduced.

--- a/net/tcp/tcp_input.c
+++ b/net/tcp/tcp_input.c
@@ -1230,9 +1230,22 @@
 
       if (TCP_SEQ_LTE(ackseq, unackseq))
         {
+          uint32_t ackedseq = TCP_SEQ_SUB(unackseq, lasttxunacked);
+
+          /* Retransmission can reduce tx_unacked to the replayed flight,
+           * without reducing the highest sequence sent. Do not infer the
+           * cumulative ACK edge from those two different quantities.
+           * After the handshake, snd_wl2 records the latest accepted ACK.
+           */
+
+          if ((conn->tcpstateflags & TCP_STATE_MASK) >= TCP_ESTABLISHED)
+            {
+              ackedseq = conn->snd_wl2;
+            }
+
           /* Calculate the new number of outstanding, unacknowledged bytes */
 
-          if (conn->tx_unacked < unackseq - ackseq)
+          if (TCP_SEQ_LT(ackseq, ackedseq))
             {
               /* old ack */
 
